                    <occupation>umbrella manufacturer</occupation>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>John Fairley was an Edinburgh businessman who helped Godwin to establish a sales network for
      his books in Scotland. He also manufactured umbrellas. Godwin wrote a substantial number of letters to him, 
      particularly in 1811 when Godwin's step-son, <persName key="CLA02">Charles Clairmont</persName>, went to 
      Edinburgh to work with <persName key="CON01">Archibald Constable</persName>, with Fairley taking him in and providing him lodgings when he first arrived in Edinburgh.</p>
                        <p>Godwin spelled this name Fairly for much of the diary, although in 1811 it looks like he switched from Fairly
    to Fairley, and then back again. These have all been coded as John Fairley.</p>
                    </note>
